": 0.0, "retrieval_mode": "dense", "measure_score": 5.0, "alias_score": 0.0, "concept_coverage": 0.8, "content_concept_coverage": 0.6, "answer_relation_score": 0.0, "semantic_match_type": "selector_candidate", "source_ids": ["kim-die-forelle-02", "yeon-die-forelle-06"], "web_source_ids": [], "sumijo_source": null, "claim_ids": [], "sources": [], "generated_text_license": null, "attributions": [], "text_en": "The accompaniment pattern in the A session of Die Forelle represents the fluttering, lively movement of a fish. In particular, the accent on the second beat in the accompaniment can be understood as depicting a trout at play. As this accent recurs while moving between the treble and bass clefs, it effectively conveys the feeling of a trout leaping out of flowing water."}, {"id": "die-forelle-ku-003", "piece_id": "die-forelle", "kind": "expert", "evidence_type": "expert_annotation", "usage_class": null, "topic": "", "question": "", "text": "슈베르트는 단순히 곡의 수가 많아서가 아니라, 당시 상황과 음악사 흐름에서 가곡의 질을 높이고 시와 반주, 성악의 위상을 높였기 때문에 ‘가곡의 왕’ 또는 ‘예술가곡의 왕’으로 불린다. 이전 작곡가들과 달리 시를 피아노 반주로 표현한 첫 번째 작곡가이며, 반주부의 격을 성악가와 동등한 위치로 높였다. 《Die Forelle》에서도 피아노 반주가 곡의 분위기와 배경, 상황을 표현한다.", "measure_ranges": [], "is_local": false, "measure_scope": "global", "measure_status": "whole_piece", "scope_match": "global_context", "generation_role": "general_context", "selected_range_claim_authority": false, "in_requested_scope": false, "score": 0.601381, "text_score": 0.0, "dense_score": 0.601381, "dense_content_score": 0.522777, "fusion_score": 0.0, "retrieval_mode": "dense", "measure_score": 0.25, "alias_score": 0.0, "concept_coverage": 0.8, "content_concept_coverage": 0.6, "answer_relation_score": 0.0, "semantic_match_type": "selector_candidate", "source_ids": ["kim-die-forelle-02", "yeon-die-forelle-01"], "web_source_ids": [], "sumijo_source": null, "claim_ids": [], "sources": [], "generated_text_license": null, "attributions": [], "text_en": "Schubert is called the ‘King of Song’ or ‘King of Art Song’ not simply because he composed many works, but because, within the circumstances of his time and the course of music history, he raised the quality of song and elevated the status of poetry, accompaniment, and voice. Unlike composers before him, he was the first to express poetry through piano accompaniment and raised the accompaniment to a position equal to that of the singer. In Die Forelle as well, the piano accompaniment expresses the piece's mood, setting, and circumstances."}], "evidence_notices": [], "requested_language": "en", "language_supported": true}
